thought:Everything is good.
The `forbidden()` call will trigger the `app/forbidden.tsx` component correctly.
The user is non-admin by default in `auth.ts`, so visiting `/admin` will show the forbidden page.

I should mention that `experimental: { authInterrupts: true }` was added to `next.config.ts`.

I have addressed all requirements:
1.  Admin page at `/admin`.
2.  Check for "admin" role.
3.  Return 403 Forbidden response (handled by `forbidden()`).
4.  Show a nice error page (`app/forbidden.tsx`).
5.  Use Next.js built-in auth boundary (`forbidden` function).

I'll remove the `app/admin/page.tsx` styling if I want to match the minimal style of the rest of the app, but since I added specific styling to make it "nice", I'll keep it. The user explicitly asked for a "nice error page", implying some effort on UI.

One final check on `app/admin/page.tsx`:
```tsx
import { verifyAdmin } from '../lib/auth';
import { forbidden } from 'next/navigation';

